; host/decimal.bl — the pinned HOST MODULE for `Elixir.BeamLisp.Decimal`.
;
; `BeamLisp.Decimal` is the exact decimal: an unbounded integer `unscaled` and
; a non-negative integer `scale`, denoting `unscaled × 10⁻ˢᶜᵃˡᵉ`. `1.50M` is
; `{150, 2}` and `1.5M` is `{15, 1}`: numerically equal, NOT `=`-equal, exactly
; `java.math.BigDecimal` — the scale IS information (cents vs. tenths) and is
; never silently dropped. This file is the `.bl` port of
; `lib/beam_lisp/decimal.ex`, compiled by `BeamLisp.AOT/compile_host_file` into
; ONE exact-named beam whose `__struct__/0,1` pair and `__info__/1` are
; SYNTHESIZED by the `:struct_fields` option (`[{:unscaled 0} {:scale 0}]`,
; declaration order) — no `defstruct`, no Elixir source.
;
; THE EXPORT SURFACE IS THE LIVE ONE PLUS THIS FILE'S HELPERS. A pinned module
; exports exactly its file's defns, so the helpers `decimal.ex` keeps private
; (`align`, `round_div`, `pow10`, `strip_factor`, `float_to_ratio`,
; `scale_pow2`, `signed`, `exact_suffix`, `bignum_magnitude`,
; `normalize_exponent`, `compare_to_float`) are exports on this beam, as are the
; small construction helpers below. That is the ONE deliberate divergence, the
; same one `priv/host/vector.bl` carries for its trie helpers: the PUBLIC ABI
; (`new/1` `of/2` `from_float/1` `parse/1` `parse!/1` `zero/0` `one/0` `ten/0`
; `add/2` `sub/2` `mul/2` `neg/1` `abs/1` `signum/1` `div/2` `div/4` `rescale/2`
; `rescale/3` `strip_zeros/1` `move_point_left/2` `move_point_right/2`
; `compare/2` `eq?/2` `scale/1` `unscaled/1` `precision/1` `to_integer/1`
; `to_float/1` `to_plain_string/1` `to_literal/1` `codec_key_parts/1`
; `modes/0` plus the synthesised struct pair, `__info__/1` and
; `MACRO-is_decimal/2`) is exact.
;
; `is_decimal/1` IS PORTED, and as the compile-time PAIR the live `defguard`
; generates rather than as a runtime function: the implementer
; `MACRO-is_decimal/2` — Elixir applies it to `(caller-env, argument-ast)` when a
; `require`d caller compiles `Decimal.is_decimal(x)`, and its RETURN VALUE is
; the quoted guard the caller compiles — plus the `__info__(:macros)` entry
; `{is_decimal, 1}`, declared through `compile_host_file`'s `:macro_exports`
; option. Nothing in the tree imports the Elixir macro (`rt.bl` writes the tag
; test by hand instead) and `decimal?/1` is still the PREDICATE a BL caller
; reaches for, but the public ABI keeps both. The section “the
; `defguard is_decimal/1` macro ABI” below states what the implementer returns
; and where it deliberately parts from Elixir's byte shape.
;
; THE FILE NAMES NO MODULE. The struct it builds is tagged with the atom THIS
; source was compiled into, read from a fun literal compiled into this file
; (`erlang/fun_info(F, :module)` — the `priv/host/vector.bl` / `delay.bl`
; idiom), so the same bytes compiled at `Elixir.BeamLisp.Decimal` build a
; production decimal and the same bytes at a probe atom build that probe's.
; `decimal?/1` is the gate: a struct tagged for THIS beam OR for the production
; `Elixir.BeamLisp.Decimal` is a decimal, and every field is read with
; `maps/get`, never by tag dispatch — so one source answers the value it built
; and the value the seed's production beam built, while a plain map, a list, an
; integer or a foreign struct is refused with the SAME `FunctionClauseError` the
; live `%__MODULE__{}` clause heads raised.
;
; DEPENDENCY SHAPE — the boot edge the migration had to keep. `rt.bl`, `num.bl`
; and the reader all reach this module (`rt` at runtime, `num` from the boot
; tier, the reader through `parse/1` while READING any file, boot tier
; included), so nothing here may depend on a namespace that is interned later.
; The whole file is therefore written against `:erlang` BIFs, `:lists`/`:binary`
; and the Elixir standard library (`Elixir.Integer`, `Elixir.String`,
; `Elixir.Float`, `Elixir.List`, `Elixir.Regex`, `Elixir.Kernel`,
; `Elixir.Map`) plus the pinned `BeamLisp.ExInfo` — itself installed by
; `Bootstrap.install_hosts!/1` BEFORE the boot tier is interned. NO beam-lisp
; namespace is named, with exactly ONE inherited exception: `new/1`'s coerce
; message calls `BeamLisp.Ns.Rt/print_str`, which is what the live
; `decimal.ex` calls. That dependency is PRESERVED, not introduced — and it sits
; on the unreachable-during-boot error path (constructing a decimal from a
; non-number), while `parse/1` and `parse!/1`, the reader's own entry points,
; need nothing but the runtime and the standard library.
;
; ARITHMETIC IS THE ERLANG BIF, NEVER THE LANGUAGE OPERATOR. Every body here
; computes on integers with `erlang/+` `erlang/-` `erlang/*` `erlang/div`
; `erlang/rem` `erlang/abs`. A body spelled `(+ a b)` would be LOWERED to the
; same `:arith` node a caller's `(+ a b)` gets, which dispatches through
; `BeamLisp.Num` — the very module that calls INTO this one the moment an
; operand is a decimal. `erlang/+` is a direct BIF call: no re-entry, and the
; same `:badarith` for an operand the BIF refuses.
;
; TWO BEHAVIOURS DIVERGE, both deliberately, both pinned by the test:
;
;   1. `move_point_left/2` and `move_point_right/2` MUTUALLY RECURSE in the
;      live module when the first operand is not a decimal and the second IS an
;      integer: clause 2 of each delegates to the other with a negated count,
;      with no base case for a non-decimal, so `Decimal.move_point_left(:x, 1)`
;      LOOPS FOREVER (measured: it is two tail calls, so it spins without
;      growing the heap). This port raises the `FunctionClauseError` the clause
;      heads promise instead. The path is unreachable from the language — the
;      seed library's `decimal/move-point-left` converts with `of` first, which
;      refuses `:x` with `:decimal/coerce` — so the choice is between an
;      infinite loop and the error the clauses describe, and an infinite loop
;      is not a behaviour worth preserving.
;
;   2. A `%BeamLisp.Decimal{}` built by THIS beam carries THIS beam's tag, so a
;      probe-built decimal and a production-built one are not `=` — the same
;      probe/production tag split `priv/host/vector.bl` documents. The field
;      values, the struct's `__info__(:struct)` metadata and every answer
;      computed from the fields are identical.
;
; (This wave owns source and tests only; the seed entry that points this file at
; `Elixir.BeamLisp.Decimal` — `:struct_fields [{:unscaled 0} {:scale 0}]`,
; `:macro_exports [{:is_decimal 1}]` and no `:behaviour` — is written by the
; wave that deletes `decimal.ex`, the family convention every pinned port in
; `priv/host/` follows.)
;
; Run: bin/bl test test/bl/host_decimal_test.bl

(ns host.decimal)

; ── module-exact self reference ───────────────────────────────────────────

(defn- self_mod
  "The atom THIS source was compiled into, read from a fun literal compiled into
   this file — module-exact, so the same source serves the production atom in
   the seed and a probe atom under test without naming either."
  []
  (erlang/element 2 (erlang/fun_info (fn [] nil) :module)))

; ── tuples and lists from `:erlang` primitives only ───────────────────────
;
; beam-lisp's `(tuple …)`/`(list …)` are `BeamLisp.Ns.Core` calls, and Core is a
; boot namespace reached while the boot tier is still being interned — the rule
; `priv/host/vector.bl` holds to for the same reason. These build the same data
; out of `make_tuple`/`setelement`/`tuple_to_list`.

(defn- tup2 [a b]
  (erlang/setelement 2 (erlang/setelement 1 (erlang/make_tuple 2 :undefined) a) b))

(defn- tup3 [a b c]
  (erlang/setelement 3
    (erlang/setelement 2
      (erlang/setelement 1 (erlang/make_tuple 3 :undefined) a) b) c))

(defn- tup4 [a b c d]
  (erlang/setelement 4
    (erlang/setelement 3
      (erlang/setelement 2
        (erlang/setelement 1 (erlang/make_tuple 4 :undefined) a) b) c) d))

(defn- tup8 [a b c d e f g h]
  (erlang/setelement 8
    (erlang/setelement 7
      (erlang/setelement 6
        (erlang/setelement 5
          (erlang/setelement 4
            (erlang/setelement 3
              (erlang/setelement 2
                (erlang/setelement 1 (erlang/make_tuple 8 :undefined) a) b) c) d) e) f) g) h))

(defn- singleton [x]
  (erlang/setelement 1 (erlang/make_tuple 1 :undefined) x))

(defn- lst1 [a] (erlang/tuple_to_list (singleton a)))
(defn- lst2 [a b] (erlang/tuple_to_list (tup2 a b)))
(defn- lst3 [a b c] (erlang/tuple_to_list (tup3 a b c)))
(defn- lst4 [a b c d] (erlang/tuple_to_list (tup4 a b c d)))

(defn- cons
  "`[x | xs]`, without the beam-lisp `cons` var (a `Core` call)."
  [x xs]
  (erlang/++ (lst1 x) xs))

(defn- bin1
  "A one-byte binary holding `b`."
  [b]
  (erlang/list_to_binary (lst1 b)))

(defn- cat2 [a b]
  (erlang/iolist_to_binary (lst2 a b)))

(defn- cat3 [a b c]
  (erlang/iolist_to_binary (lst3 a b c)))

(defn- cat4 [a b c d]
  (erlang/iolist_to_binary (lst4 a b c d)))

; ── errors ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(defn- clause_error
  "The `FunctionClauseError` an unmatched clause head raised, named with the
   FUNCTION and ARITY of the clause that failed — so a failure inside a helper
   reads exactly as the live module's did (`add/2` reports `align/2`, because
   that is the clause head that actually failed)."
  [fname arity]
  (erlang/error
    (Elixir.FunctionClauseError/exception
      (lst3 (tup2 :module (self_mod))
            (tup2 :function fname)
            (tup2 :arity arity)))))

(defn- ex_info
  "A `BeamLisp.ExInfo` with `message` and `data` — the exception every refusal
   in this module raises, class for class with the live one."
  [message data]
  (BeamLisp.ExInfo/exception
    (lst2 (tup2 :message message) (tup2 :data data))))

(defn- data1 [k v] (maps/put k v (maps/new)))

(defn- data2 [k1 v1 k2 v2] (maps/put k2 v2 (maps/put k1 v1 (maps/new))))

; ── the struct: gated read, module-tagged construction ────────────────────

(defn- decimal?
  "`%__MODULE__{}`-shaped input: a map whose atom `:__struct__` tag is THIS
   beam's module or the production `Elixir.BeamLisp.Decimal`. The two-atom gate
   is what lets the SAME source accept the value it built (probe-tagged) and the
   value the seed's production beam built, while refusing a plain map, a list or
   a foreign struct exactly as the live clause heads did. Fields are then read
   with `maps/get`, which never dispatches through the tag."
  [x]
  (if (erlang/is_map x)
    (if (erlang/is_map_key :__struct__ x)
      (let [tag (erlang/map_get :__struct__ x)]
        (or (erlang/== tag :"Elixir.BeamLisp.Decimal")
            (erlang/== tag (self_mod))))
      false)
    false))

(defn- struct_of
  "The decimal struct `{unscaled, scale}`, tagged with THIS beam's module — the
   shape the synthesized `__struct__/1` would build, assembled without naming
   it."
  [unscaled scale]
  (maps/put :__struct__ (self_mod)
    (maps/put :unscaled unscaled
      (maps/put :scale scale (maps/new)))))

(defn- field [k d] (maps/get k d))

; ── the `defguard is_decimal/1` macro ABI ────────────────────────────────
;
; The live module declares the guard with `defguard`, whose beam artifacts are
; a compile-time PAIR rather than a runtime function: the implementer
; `MACRO-is_decimal/2` — Elixir applies it to `(caller-env, argument-ast)` when
; a `require`d caller compiles `Decimal.is_decimal(x)`, and its RETURN VALUE is
; the quoted guard that caller then compiles — and the `__info__(:macros)`
; entry `{is_decimal, 1}`, without which `require` refuses to expand the call at
; all. The entry is declared through `compile_host_file`'s `:macro_exports`
; option (the seed entry names it); the implementer is below.
;
; THE GUARD IT RETURNS is `is_map(x) andalso is_map_key(:__struct__, x) andalso
; map_get(:__struct__, x) == THIS MODULE` — the same expression `Kernel.is_struct/2`
; expands to for a `defguard`, and the only reading of the live guard's
; semantics (`defguard is_decimal(x) when is_struct(x, __MODULE__)`) that is also
; a legal Elixir GUARD: every call in it is a guard BIF, `andalso`
; SHORT-CIRCUITS (so `map_get/2` is reached only once `x` is known to be a
; `:__struct__`-carrying map, and a non-map fails the guard instead of raising
; `badmap`), and every node it builds is DATA — an AST for the CALLER's compiler
; to expand, never a call made here, so no `BeamLisp.Ns.Core`/runtime var appears
; in it.
;
; TWO DELIBERATE DIVERGENCES from the byte shape Elixir emits, and no others:
;
;   * Elixir's expansion also carries a compile-time-only assertion of the
;     module atom (`:erlang.orelse(:erlang.is_atom(Decimal), fail)`). It is dead
;     code by construction — it asserts a literal the caller cannot vary — so it
;     is elided.
;   * In a GUARD the argument AST is spliced in once per test, exactly as Elixir
;     does: a guard is pure, so re-reading the argument cannot change the
;     answer. OUTSIDE a guard a second read of a side-effecting argument WOULD
;     be observable, so the value is bound once to a hygienic variable and the
;     tests read THAT — Elixir's own two-branch shape, selected by
;     `Macro.Env.in_guard?/1` on the caller environment.
;
; The caller is what Elixir passes FIRST to every macro implementer;
; `elixir_env/to_caller` is Elixir's own normalizer for its two shapes (the
; compiler's `{line, compile-state, %Macro.Env{}}`, a tool's bare
; `%Macro.Env{}`) and is exactly what `defguard`'s generated code calls, while
; `Elixir.Macro.Env/in_guard?/1` is public API. This function runs only when an
; Elixir compiler expands the macro — never on the boot path the rest of this
; file is held to — which is why it may name them.

(defn- ast_node
  "One Elixir AST node: `{form, meta, args}`. A macro's contract is a TERM, not
   a call, so this is plain tuple construction and not a `BeamLisp.Ns.Core`
   `(tuple …)`."
  [form meta args]
  (erlang/list_to_tuple (lst3 form meta args)))

(defn- ast_call
  "The Elixir AST for the remote call `mod.fun(args)`:
   `{{:., [], [mod, fun]}, [], args}`. `meta` is stamped on BOTH nodes — which
   is how `generated: true` reaches the compiler (see `bind_once_ast`)."
  [meta mod fun args]
  (ast_node (ast_node :"." meta (lst2 mod fun)) meta args))

(defn- is_decimal_ast
  "The guard expression recognising a decimal, with `x` the argument AST and
   `meta` stamped on every node it builds. `andalso` short-circuits, so
   `:erlang.map_get/2` is reached only after `x` is known to be a
   `:__struct__`-carrying map — the same order, and the same freedom from
   `badmap`, as `Kernel.is_struct/2`."
  [meta x]
  (ast_call meta :erlang :andalso
    (lst2 (ast_call meta :erlang :andalso
             (lst2 (ast_call meta :erlang :is_map (lst1 x))
                   (ast_call meta :erlang :is_map_key (lst2 :__struct__ x))))
          (ast_call meta :erlang :==
            (lst2 (ast_call meta :erlang :map_get (lst2 :__struct__ x))
                  (self_mod))))))

(defn- bound_var_ast
  "The variable a macro introduces: `{arg1, [generated: true], THIS MODULE}`.
   The module as the name's CONTEXT is what makes it hygienic — it cannot
   collide with a caller's own `arg1` — and `generated` keeps the compiler from
   warning about a binding the caller never wrote."
  []
  (ast_node :arg1 (lst1 (tup2 :generated true)) (self_mod)))

(defn- bind_once_ast
  "The NON-GUARD expansion: read the argument ONCE, then run the guard tests on
   the binding. See the section comment for why this branch exists.

   Every node it builds is stamped `generated: true`. That is Elixir's own mark
   for code the caller did not write, and it is load-bearing rather than
   cosmetic: `:erlang.andalso/2` is an OPERATOR, not an exported function, so a
   remote call to it in a non-guard position is `undefined or private` as far as
   the compiler's function check is concerned and earns a warning — one the
   reference expansion never shows because all of its generated nodes carry the
   same stamp."
  [x]
  (let [gen (lst1 (tup2 :generated true))
        v (bound_var_ast)]
    (ast_node :__block__ gen
      (lst2 (ast_node := gen (lst2 v x))
            (is_decimal_ast gen v)))))

(defn MACRO-is_decimal
  "`defguard is_decimal/1`'s implementer: `(caller-env, argument-ast)` in, the
   quoted guard out, for the caller's compiler to expand. The `defguard`
   predicate is `is_struct(x, __MODULE__)`; see the section comment above for
   what is returned, and for the two deliberate divergences from the bytes
   Elixir emits."
  [caller x]
  (let [env (elixir_env/to_caller caller)]
    (if (Elixir.Macro.Env/in_guard? env)
      (is_decimal_ast (quote ()) x)
      (bind_once_ast x))))

; ── construction ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(defn modes
  "The rounding modes, as keywords — `@modes` in the live module, in the same
   order, built here without a Core list literal."
  []
  (erlang/tuple_to_list
    (tup8 :half-even :half-up :half-down :ceiling :floor :down :up :unnecessary)))

(defn- valid_mode? [mode]
  (lists/member mode (modes)))

(defn new
  "A decimal from an integer (scale 0), a string (`\"123.45\"`, `\"-1e3\"`), or a
   decimal (identity). A FLOAT IS REFUSED, and so is anything else — the live
   `new/1` has a clause for each and a final clause that raises `ExInfo`, so it
   never reports a clause failure."
  [x]
  (cond
    (decimal? x) x
    (erlang/is_integer x) (struct_of x 0)
    (erlang/is_binary x) (parse! x)
    (erlang/is_float x) (inexact! x)
    :else (coerce! x)))

(defn- inexact!
  "`:decimal/inexact-operand` — a binary float is not the number a programmer
   wrote (`0.1` is not one tenth), so coercing it silently would launder that
   error into `exact` arithmetic. `decimal/from-float` is the deliberate door."
  [f]
  (erlang/error
    (ex_info
      (cat3 "a float is not an exact decimal: " (Kernel/inspect f)
            " (use decimal/from-float to approximate on purpose)")
      (data2 :type :"decimal/inexact-operand" :value f))))

(defn- coerce!
  "`:decimal/coerce` — the printed form of the offending value, exactly as the
   live module prints it. `BeamLisp.Ns.Rt/print_str` is the live module's OWN
   dependency, preserved here verbatim (see the header): this path is only
   reachable once the runtime is interned, and nothing on the boot path can
   reach it."
  [x]
  (erlang/error
    (ex_info
      (cat2 "cannot make a decimal from " (BeamLisp.Ns.Rt/print_str x))
      (data2 :type :"decimal/coerce" :value x))))

(defn of
  "A decimal from an unscaled integer and a scale — the raw constructor. Any
   other pair is the live `FunctionClauseError`."
  ([unscaled scale] :when (and (int? unscaled) (int? scale) (>= scale 0))
   (struct_of unscaled scale))
  ([unscaled scale]
   (clause_error :of 2)))

(defn from_float
  "A decimal from a float via its shortest round-tripping decimal expansion."
  ([f] :when (float? f) (parse! (Float/to_string f)))
  ([f] (clause_error :from_float 1)))

(defn parse
  "`\"[+-]digits[.digits][e[+-]digits]\"` — `{:ok, d}` or `:error`. The pattern
   is the live one, run through `Elixir.Regex` so the tokenisation is the same
   engine: the reader has no hash-quote regex literal, so a pinned file cannot
   hoist a compiled pattern into the beam as a constant and compiles it per
   call. `Regex.run` omits TRAILING unmatched groups, so the four captures are
   padded back to four before they are read."
  ([s] :when (string? s)
   (let [m (Regex/run
             (Regex/compile! "\\A\\s*([+-])?(\\d*)(?:\\.(\\d*))?(?:[eE]([+-]?\\d+))?\\s*\\z")
             s)]
     (if (erlang/== nil m)
       :error
       (let [caps0 (erlang/tl m)
             n (erlang/length caps0)
             caps (if (erlang/< n 4)
                    (erlang/++ caps0 (List/duplicate "" (erlang/- 4 n)))
                    caps0)
             sign (lists/nth 1 caps)
             int (lists/nth 2 caps)
             frac (lists/nth 3 caps)
             exp0 (lists/nth 4 caps)]
         (if (and (erlang/== int "") (erlang/== frac ""))
           :error
           (let [e (if (erlang/== exp0 "") 0 (String/to_integer exp0))
                 d0 (String/to_integer (cat2 int frac))
                 d (if (erlang/== sign "-") (erlang/- 0 d0) d0)]
             (tup2 :ok (normalize_exponent d (erlang/- (erlang/byte_size frac) e)))))))))
  ([s] (clause_error :parse 1)))

(defn parse!
  "`parse/1`, raising `:decimal/parse` instead of answering `:error`."
  [s]
  (let [r (parse s)]
    (if (erlang/== :error r)
      (erlang/error
        (ex_info (cat2 "not a decimal: " (Kernel/inspect s))
                 (data2 :type :"decimal/parse" :value s)))
      (erlang/element 2 r))))

(defn- normalize_exponent
  "A negative scale (from an exponent) is folded into the unscaled value:
   `1e3` is `{1000, 0}` — the scale never goes below zero here."
  [digits scale]
  (if (erlang/>= scale 0)
    (of digits scale)
    (of (erlang/* digits (pow10 (erlang/- 0 scale))) 0)))

(defn zero [] (of 0 0))
(defn one [] (of 1 0))
(defn ten [] (of 10 0))

; ── exact arithmetic ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(defn align
  "Both unscaled values at the larger scale. A non-decimal operand is the
   `FunctionClauseError` this clause head raised in the live module — the
   failure `add/2` and `sub/2` reported, because this is the head that failed."
  [a b]
  (if (and (decimal? a) (decimal? b))
    (let [sa (field :scale a)
          sb (field :scale b)
          s (erlang/max sa sb)]
      (tup3 (erlang/* (field :unscaled a) (pow10 (erlang/- s sa)))
            (erlang/* (field :unscaled b) (pow10 (erlang/- s sb)))
            s))
    (clause_error :align 2)))

(defn add [a b]
  (let [al (align a b)]
    (of (erlang/+ (erlang/element 1 al) (erlang/element 2 al)) (erlang/element 3 al))))

(defn sub [a b]
  (let [al (align a b)]
    (of (erlang/- (erlang/element 1 al) (erlang/element 2 al)) (erlang/element 3 al))))

(defn mul
  "Exact product: the scale is the SUM of the operands' scales."
  [a b]
  (if (and (decimal? a) (decimal? b))
    (of (erlang/* (field :unscaled a) (field :unscaled b))
        (erlang/+ (field :scale a) (field :scale b)))
    (clause_error :mul 2)))

(defn neg [d]
  (if (decimal? d)
    (struct_of (erlang/- 0 (field :unscaled d)) (field :scale d))
    (clause_error :neg 1)))

(defn abs [d]
  (if (decimal? d)
    (struct_of (erlang/abs (field :unscaled d)) (field :scale d))
    (clause_error :abs 1)))

(defn signum
  "-1, 0 or 1 — the sign, scale-insensitive."
  [d]
  (if (decimal? d)
    (let [u (field :unscaled d)]
      (cond
        (erlang/> u 0) 1
        (erlang/< u 0) -1
        :else 0))
    (clause_error :signum 1)))

; ── rounding ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(defn div
  "`div/4`: `a` ÷ `b` to exactly `scale` fractional digits under `mode` — the
   one division that rounds. `div/2` is EXACT division: the quotient must
   terminate, else `:decimal/non-terminating`. Division by zero raises
   `:decimal/divide-by-zero` in both."
  ([a b]
   (if (and (decimal? a) (decimal? b))
     (let [bu (field :unscaled b)]
       (if (erlang/== bu 0)
         (divide_by_zero! a)
         ; ua/ub terminates iff, after cancelling the gcd, the denominator has
         ; no prime factor besides 2 and 5; the digits needed is
         ; max(#2s, #5s). So the exact scale is decidable up front — no
         ; searching, no guessed bound.
         (let [g (Integer/gcd (field :unscaled a) bu)
               den (erlang/abs (erlang/div bu g))
               t (strip_factor den 2 0)
               twos (erlang/element 1 t)
               d1 (erlang/element 2 t)
               f (strip_factor d1 5 0)
               fives (erlang/element 1 f)
               d2 (erlang/element 2 f)]
           (if (erlang/=/= d2 1)
             (non_terminating! a b)
             ; exact at (sa − sb) + max(twos, fives) fractional digits; never
             ; below the preferred scale sa − sb (clamped at 0).
             (let [preferred (erlang/- (field :scale a) (field :scale b))]
               (div a b (erlang/max (erlang/+ preferred (erlang/max twos fives)) 0)
                    :unnecessary))))))
     (clause_error :div 2)))
  ([a b scale mode]
   (if (and (decimal? a) (decimal? b) (int? scale) (>= scale 0)
            (valid_mode? mode))
     (let [bu (field :unscaled b)]
       (if (erlang/== bu 0)
         (divide_by_zero! a)
         ; a/b at `scale` digits = (ua · 10^(scale + sb − sa)) / ub, rounded.
         (let [shift (erlang/- (erlang/+ scale (field :scale b)) (field :scale a))
               nd (if (erlang/>= shift 0)
                    (tup2 (erlang/* (field :unscaled a) (pow10 shift)) bu)
                    (tup2 (field :unscaled a) (erlang/* bu (pow10 (erlang/- 0 shift)))))]
           (of (round_div (erlang/element 1 nd) (erlang/element 2 nd) mode) scale))))
     (clause_error :div 4))))

(defn- divide_by_zero!
  [dividend]
  (erlang/error
    (ex_info "decimal division by zero"
             (data2 :type :"decimal/divide-by-zero" :dividend dividend))))

(defn- non_terminating!
  [dividend divisor]
  (erlang/error
    (ex_info "non-terminating decimal expansion; no exact representable decimal result"
             (maps/put :type :"decimal/non-terminating"
               (maps/put :dividend dividend
                 (maps/put :divisor divisor (maps/new)))))))

(defn rescale
  "`the same number at another scale`: growing the scale is exact; shrinking it
   rounds under `mode`. `rescale/2` defaults to `:unnecessary`, which refuses
   rather than lose a digit."
  ([d scale]
   (if (and (decimal? d) (erlang/is_integer scale))
     (rescale d scale :unnecessary)
     (clause_error :rescale 2)))
  ([d scale mode]
   (if (and (decimal? d) (int? scale) (>= scale 0) (valid_mode? mode))
     (let [s (field :scale d)]
       (cond
         (erlang/== scale s) d
         (erlang/> scale s)
         (of (erlang/* (field :unscaled d) (pow10 (erlang/- scale s))) scale)
         :else
         (of (round_div (field :unscaled d) (pow10 (erlang/- s scale)) mode) scale)))
     (clause_error :rescale 3))))

(defn strip_zeros
  "`stripTrailingZeros`: the smallest scale representing the same number."
  [d]
  (if (decimal? d)
    (let [u (field :unscaled d)
          s (field :scale d)]
      (if (erlang/== u 0)
        (zero)
        (if (and (erlang/> s 0) (erlang/== 0 (erlang/rem u 10)))
          (strip_zeros (of (erlang/div u 10) (erlang/- s 1)))
          d)))
    (clause_error :strip_zeros 1)))

(defn move_point_left
  "`movePointLeft(n)`: divide by 10ⁿ exactly (the scale grows by n)."
  [d n]
  (if (decimal? d)
    (if (and (erlang/is_integer n) (erlang/>= n 0))
      (of (field :unscaled d) (erlang/+ (field :scale d) n))
      (if (erlang/is_integer n)
        (move_point_right d (erlang/- 0 n))
        (clause_error :move_point_left 2)))
    ; NOT the live mutual recursion: see the header. A non-decimal operand has
    ; no clause here, and the live clause 2 has no base case for one.
    (clause_error :move_point_left 2)))

(defn move_point_right
  "`movePointRight(n)`: multiply by 10ⁿ exactly."
  [d n]
  (if (decimal? d)
    (if (and (erlang/is_integer n) (erlang/>= n 0))
      (let [s (field :scale d)]
        (if (erlang/=< n s)
          (of (field :unscaled d) (erlang/- s n))
          (of (erlang/* (field :unscaled d) (pow10 (erlang/- n s))) 0)))
      (if (erlang/is_integer n)
        (move_point_left d (erlang/- 0 n))
        (clause_error :move_point_right 2)))
    (clause_error :move_point_right 2)))

(defn round_div
  "Integer division of `num` by `den` under a rounding mode. Written once over
   `div`/`rem` so every mode is one table, not seven near-copies. `:unnecessary`
   raises `:decimal/rounding-necessary`."
  [num den mode]
  (let [q (erlang/div num den)
        r (erlang/rem num den)]
    (if (erlang/== r 0)
      q
      (let [negative (erlang/=/= (erlang/< num 0) (erlang/< den 0))
            ; twice the remainder against the divisor decides the half cases
            half (erlang/* (erlang/abs r) 2)
            cmp (erlang/abs den)
            away (if negative (erlang/- q 1) (erlang/+ q 1))
            toward_zero q]
        (cond
          (erlang/== mode :unnecessary)
          (erlang/error
            (ex_info "rounding necessary" (data1 :type :"decimal/rounding-necessary")))

          (erlang/== mode :down) toward_zero
          (erlang/== mode :up) away
          (erlang/== mode :floor) (if negative away toward_zero)
          (erlang/== mode :ceiling) (if negative toward_zero away)
          (erlang/== mode :half-up) (if (erlang/>= half cmp) away toward_zero)
          (erlang/== mode :half-down) (if (erlang/> half cmp) away toward_zero)
          ; `:half-even` is the `case`'s last clause: an already-validated mode
          ; that reached here IS half-even.
          :else
          (cond
            (erlang/> half cmp) away
            (erlang/< half cmp) toward_zero
            (erlang/== 0 (erlang/rem q 2)) toward_zero
            :else away))))))

; ── comparison ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(defn compare
  "Numeric comparison (scale-insensitive): -1, 0, 1. Accepts an integer on
   either side — anything else goes through `new/1`, so a value that is not
   coercible reports `:decimal/coerce` exactly as the live module did."
  [a b]
  (let [al (align (new a) (new b))
        ua (erlang/element 1 al)
        ub (erlang/element 2 al)]
    (cond
      (erlang/< ua ub) -1
      (erlang/> ua ub) 1
      :else 0)))

(defn eq?
  "Numerically equal? `1.0M == 1.00M`, unlike `=`."
  [a b]
  (erlang/== 0 (compare a b)))

; ── conversion ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(defn scale [d]
  (if (decimal? d) (field :scale d) (clause_error :scale 1)))

(defn unscaled [d]
  (if (decimal? d) (field :unscaled d) (clause_error :unscaled 1)))

(defn precision
  "Number of significant digits (`precision()`); 1 for zero."
  [d]
  (if (decimal? d)
    (let [u (field :unscaled d)]
      (if (erlang/== u 0)
        1
        (erlang/length (Integer/digits (erlang/abs u)))))
    (clause_error :precision 1)))

(defn to_integer
  "`longValue`/`intValue`: the integer part, truncated toward zero."
  [d]
  (if (decimal? d)
    (field :unscaled (rescale d 0 :down))
    (clause_error :to_integer 1)))

(defn to_float
  "`doubleValue`: the nearest float — lossy by definition. A plain string that
   `Float.parse` cannot take (a value past the binary64 range) is the live
   `MatchError` on the destructuring, not a silent infinity."
  [d]
  (if (decimal? d)
    (let [r (Float/parse (to_plain_string d))]
      (if (and (erlang/is_tuple r)
               (erlang/== 2 (erlang/tuple_size r))
               (erlang/== "" (erlang/element 2 r)))
        (erlang/element 1 r)
        (erlang/error
          (Elixir.MatchError/exception
            (erlang/tuple_to_list (singleton (tup2 :term r)))))))
    (clause_error :to_float 1)))

(defn to_plain_string
  "`toPlainString`: no exponent ever, exactly `scale` fractional digits."
  [d]
  (if (decimal? d)
    (let [u (field :unscaled d)
          s (field :scale d)]
      (if (erlang/== s 0)
        (Integer/to_string u)
        (let [digits (String/pad_leading (Integer/to_string (erlang/abs u))
                                         (erlang/+ s 1) "0")
              parts (String/split_at digits (erlang/- 0 s))]
          (cat4 (if (erlang/< u 0) "-" "")
                (erlang/element 1 parts)
                "."
                (erlang/element 2 parts)))))
    (clause_error :to_plain_string 1)))

(defn to_literal
  "The reader literal form: `to_plain_string` with the `M` suffix."
  [d]
  (if (decimal? d)
    (cat2 (to_plain_string d) "M")
    (clause_error :to_literal 1)))

; ── order-preserving index key (PLAN-115 B1) ─────────────────────────────
;
; A Decimal must sort NUMERICALLY among every other number in datom's AVET
; index, and index BY NUMERIC VALUE — `1.0M` and `1.00M` collide, as
; Datomic/datahike AVET does. `priv/lib/datom/codec.bl` puts every number under
; one TAG-NUM ordered by its binary64 magnitude, with an exact tie-break suffix
; for values that share a float; THIS is the decimal half of that scheme:
;
;   TAG-NUM || F(q) || rel || D(x)?
;
; `q` is the decimal rounded to binary64 (codec.bl reuses its own
; `encode-float` for the 8-byte prefix, so this returns `q` rather than the
; bytes — one IEEE transform, not two), `rel` is 0/1/2 for the exact value
; BELOW/EQUAL/ABOVE its rounded float, and `D(x)` is the exact canonical suffix,
; present only when `rel ≠ 1`. The design and its ordering proof: oracle
; 9-DecimalKeyDesign, 2026-09-14.

(defn codec_key_parts
  "The order-preserving index-key BODY for a decimal: `{q_float, rel_byte,
   suffix_binary}`, to be appended after the shared `TAG-NUM || F(q)` prefix
   `codec.bl` builds."
  [d]
  (if (decimal? d)
    (let [c (strip_zeros d)
          q (to_float c)
          rel (compare_to_float c q)
          suffix (if (erlang/== rel 1) "" (exact_suffix c))]
      (tup3 q rel suffix))
    (clause_error :codec_key_parts 1)))

(defn- compare_to_float
  "Compare a decimal `u/10^s` to a finite float exactly. `q = m · 2^e`
   (`float_to_ratio/1`); crossing the two gives `u·qd` vs `qn·10^s`, an integer
   comparison with no float arithmetic anywhere."
  [d q]
  (let [u (field :unscaled d)
        s (field :scale d)
        fr (float_to_ratio q)
        qp (scale_pow2 (erlang/element 1 fr) (erlang/element 2 fr))
        left (erlang/* u (erlang/element 2 qp))
        right (erlang/* (erlang/element 1 qp) (pow10 s))]
    (cond
      (erlang/< left right) 0    ; value < q → BELOW
      (erlang/> left right) 2    ; value > q → ABOVE
      :else 1)))

(defn scale_pow2
  "`q = m·2^e` as the fraction `{numerator, denominator}`, the denominator a
   power of two (or 1)."
  [m e]
  (if (erlang/>= e 0)
    (tup2 (erlang/* m (Integer/pow 2 e)) 1)
    (tup2 m (Integer/pow 2 (erlang/- 0 e)))))

(defn float_to_ratio
  "Decompose a finite float into `{mantissa_integer, exponent}` with
   `value = m · 2^e`. The bits come from `term_to_binary/1`, whose float
   envelope (`131, 70` then eight big-endian IEEE bytes) is the portable way to
   reach them without a bitstring literal, which the reader does not provide.
   `-0.0` decomposes to `{0, 0}` — the sign of a zero is not a value here."
  [f]
  (let [bits (bits_of_float f)
        sign (erlang/bsr bits 63)
        exp (erlang/band (erlang/bsr bits 52) 2047)
        frac (erlang/band bits 4503599627370495)]
    (cond
      (and (erlang/== exp 0) (erlang/== frac 0)) (tup2 0 0)
      (erlang/== exp 0) (signed (tup2 frac -1074) sign)
      :else (signed (tup2 (erlang/+ frac 4503599627370496) (erlang/- exp 1075)) sign))))

(defn- bits_of_float
  "The raw IEEE-754 double bits of `f` as an unsigned 64-bit integer."
  [f]
  (lists/foldl (fn [b acc] (erlang/+ (erlang/* acc 256) b))
               0
               (lists/nthtail 2 (erlang/binary_to_list (erlang/term_to_binary f)))))

(defn signed
  "`{m, e}` negated when the sign bit is set."
  [me sign]
  (if (erlang/== sign 0)
    me
    (tup2 (erlang/- 0 (erlang/element 1 me)) (erlang/element 2 me))))

(defn exact_suffix
  "The exact canonical suffix `D(x)`: `floor(x)` as an ordered signed-int
   magnitude, then — when `x` is non-integral — `0xFF` and the minimal
   fractional decimal digits (each byte `digit+1`, `0x00` terminator). Matches
   `codec.bl`'s `encode-bignum-magnitude` for the integer anchor, so an integral
   decimal carries the same tie-break BYTES as the equal integer. The MARKER is
   this decimal's EXACT relation to its rounded float, which `codec.bl`'s own
   integer path derives from the sign alone; the two disagree for an integer
   whose nearest double rounds AWAY from zero (`9007199254740995` is the
   smallest) — a defect of that integer path, measured and reported with this
   port, not reproduced here."
  [d]
  (let [u (field :unscaled d)
        s (field :scale d)
        p (pow10 s)
        n (Integer/floor_div u p)
        a (erlang/- u (erlang/* n p))
        anchor (bignum_magnitude n)]
    (if (erlang/== a 0)
      anchor
      (let [digits (String/trim_trailing
                     (String/pad_leading (Integer/to_string a) s "0") "0")
            frac (erlang/list_to_binary
                   (lists/map (fn [c] (erlang/- c 47))
                              (erlang/binary_to_list digits)))]
        (cat4 anchor (bin1 255) frac (bin1 0))))))

(defn bignum_magnitude
  "Order-preserving signed-integer magnitude — the SAME format as `codec.bl`'s
   `encode-bignum-magnitude`: nonneg `1 || u32be(len) || bytes`, neg
   `0 || u32be(0xffffffff-len) || complemented bytes`."
  [n]
  (if (erlang/>= n 0)
    (let [bytes (binary/encode_unsigned n)
          len (if (erlang/== n 0) 0 (erlang/byte_size bytes))]
      (cat2 (erlang/list_to_binary (cons 1 (u32be len)))
            (if (erlang/== n 0) "" bytes)))
    (let [bytes (binary/encode_unsigned (erlang/- 0 n))
          len (erlang/byte_size bytes)
          complemented (erlang/list_to_binary
                         (lists/map (fn [b] (erlang/- 255 b))
                                    (erlang/binary_to_list bytes)))]
      (cat2 (erlang/list_to_binary (cons 0 (u32be (erlang/- 4294967295 len))))
            complemented))))

(defn- u32be
  "An unsigned 32-bit integer as four big-endian bytes."
  [n]
  (lst4 (erlang/band (erlang/bsr n 24) 255)
        (erlang/band (erlang/bsr n 16) 255)
        (erlang/band (erlang/bsr n 8) 255)
        (erlang/band n 255)))

; ── helpers ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(defn strip_factor
  "How many times `p` divides `n`, and what is left: `{k, n/p^k}`."
  [n p k]
  (if (erlang/== 0 (erlang/rem n p))
    (strip_factor (erlang/div n p) p (erlang/+ k 1))
    (tup2 k n)))

(defn pow10
  "10ⁿ for a non-negative `n`."
  [n]
  (cond
    (erlang/== n 0) 1
    (erlang/> n 0) (Integer/pow 10 n)
    :else (clause_error :pow10 1)))
